      ],
      "source": "https://www.onalaskaalliance.org/onalaska-history.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name comes from Thomas Campbell’s poem “The Pleasures of Hope,” shortened from the original Oonalaska.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.onalaskaalliance.org/onalaska-history.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "At its peak in 1929, the Carlisle mill produced 300,000 board feet of lumber daily, totaling over 20 million board feet in inventory.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Onalaska%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The last standing piece of the original mill is a smokestack built in the 1920s, now listed on the Washington Historic Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
